Lambda (Serverless Computing) :-

                 AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that lets you run code without provisioning or managing servers. You simply upload your code, define a trigger, and AWS takes care of infrastructure, scaling, availability, and patching for you.

🔹 What is Serverless?
     • No server management 🖥️❌
     • Pay only for execution time 💰
     • Automatic scaling ⚡
     • High availability by default ✅
     • You focus only on code, AWS handles the rest.

🧠 How AWS Lambda Works (Workflow)

1️⃣ Event Source An event triggers Lambda, such as:
     • API Gateway (HTTP requests)
     • S3 (file upload)
     • DynamoDB (table updates)
     • CloudWatch (scheduled jobs)
     • SQS / SNS (messages)
2️⃣ Lambda Function
     • Code written in Python, Node.js, Java, Go, etc.
     • Stateless execution (no data stored between runs)
3️⃣ Execution Environment
     • AWS spins up a secure container
     • Allocates CPU & memory automatically
     • Runs the function
4️⃣ Integration with AWS Services
     • Read/write from DynamoDB
     • Store files in S3
     • Publish logs to CloudWatch
     • Trigger other Lambdas
5️⃣ Response / Output
     • Returns response (for APIs)
     • Or completes background processing

⚙️ Key Lambda Concepts (Simple Explanation)
  🔸 Function – Your code 
  🔸 Trigger – Event that runs the function 
  🔸 Runtime – Language environment (Python, Node.js, etc.) 
  🔸 Handler – Entry point of execution 
  🔸 Timeout – Max execution time (up to 15 minutes) 
  🔸 Memory – 128 MB to 10 GB (affects CPU) 
  🔸 IAM Role – Permissions for Lambda to access AWS services

💡 Why Use AWS Lambda?
   ✅ No server management
   ✅ Auto-scaling 
   ✅ Cost-efficient (pay per execution) 
   ✅ Fast deployment
   ✅ Ideal for microservices & event-driven apps

🧪 Common Use Cases
  ✔️ REST APIs & Backend services
  ✔️ File processing (S3 triggers) 
  ✔️ Automation & cron jobs 
  ✔️ Data transformation 
  ✔️ Real-time stream processing

📌 Final Thought
                      AWS Lambda is a core building block of modern cloud-native & DevOps architectures. Mastering Lambda helps you design scalable, cost-effective, and highly available systems.
